FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1237 — The `TokenKind::Set | TokenKind::Constant` arm applies `set`'s write-through lookup to `constant` as well: `let existing = scopes.iter().rev().find_map(|scope| scope.get(name).copied());` then reuses that binding. A `constant` declared in an inner scope (loop/function/catch) where the spelling already exists in an enclosing scope is therefore never declared locally; goto/references answer the outer `set` binding and report kind `variable`, not `constant`. The comment above only justifies write-through for `set`. Fix: restrict the enclosing-scope reuse to `TokenKind::Set`; for `Constant`, always declare in the current scope. Not verified against the interpreter: settle by running `set x to 1` then `while ... constant x to 2 ... end` and checking whether the inner use is a new constant or the outer variable.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1022 — `Catch` pushes a scope and sets `catch_scope = true` on the `Try` frame, and nothing pops it until the `end` at src/lsp.rs:892-898. The interpreter has a distinct `finally_body` (src/interpreter.rs:2510), so a `try ... catch ... finally ... end` analyzes the `finally` body inside the catch binder's scope: the catch binder remains visible in `finally`, and declarations written in `finally` are popped with the catch scope instead of staying in the enclosing scope. Fix: detect the finally clause (or the first token after the catch body) and pop the catch scope there rather than only at `End`. Settle by checking the parser keyword for finally and running a fixture where `finally` reads the catch binder and declares a name used after `end`.

3. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1020 — Modeling a `test` body as sharing the enclosing scope means every top-level `test` block now writes into the same scope: a name declared in one `test` resolves in a later `test`. The test harness extracts each body independently (src/testing/harness.rs:94) and is expected to isolate tests, so navigation can disagree with what `rb test` sees. The uncommitted change also rewrote the prior tests that asserted test-body isolation instead of supplementing them, so nothing pins the new model. Fix: keep `test` bodies out of the file-level scope (or scope them per test) unless the harness provably runs all bodies in one shared environment. Settle by running two tests where one declares `x` and the other reads it under `rb test`.

